# Lief Lipsiege, the count's accountant, shackled to his desk (docs/voice/lief_lipsiege.md).
# start: at his desk. Freeing him wakes the rug (court_accountant_rug); after_rug: when the rug is gone (approach).

~ start
if flag.lief_met
Lief Lipsiege: You again. A visit from you costs me eleven minutes on average. I have opened a column for it.
-> menu
endif
set lief_met
Narrator: A thin old man in ink-stained sleeves sits at a desk heaped with ledgers. An iron shackle on his ankle is chained to the leg of the desk, and the chain has worn a groove in the floor.
Lief Lipsiege: If you are from the treasury, you are late. If you are not from the treasury, you are interrupting. Which?
-> menu

~ menu
* Who are you? -> who
* Why are you chained? -> chain
* What are you counting? -> accounts
* [if not flag.court_treasury_known] Where does the count keep his money? -> vault
* Who is in the guest rooms upstairs? -> guest
* What do you know about Rahadin? -> rahadin
* [if not flag.lief_inkwell_known] [Insight DC 12] (He keeps touching the inkwell.) -> lief_inkwell_ok | lief_inkwell_miss
* We could get you out of that chain. -> free_offer
* Goodbye. -> END

~ who
Lief Lipsiege: Lief Lipsiege. Accountant to the count, by appointment, since the year they rebuilt the bridge at Vallaki. The second time they rebuilt it.
interject tag:curious: How many times have they rebuilt it?
Lief Lipsiege: Seven. Wood is cheaper than stone and the river is patient. I have the figures.
-> menu

~ chain
Lief Lipsiege: I left once. I got as far as the gate. Rahadin brought me back, and the next morning the smith came up with this. That was forty-one years ago.
Lief Lipsiege: I have not tried again. I find the arithmetic discourages it.
-> menu

~ accounts
Lief Lipsiege: Everything. Vallaki's tithe. Wine from the vineyard, when there is wine. Rents from Krezk. Candles. Gowns. He is a very poor spender and a very good collector. It makes for dull books.
-> menu

~ vault
set court_treasury_known
Lief Lipsiege: Behind the lady in the long hall, of course. The alcove. Push the left side of the frame. He likes her to keep an eye on it.
Lief Lipsiege [afraid]: Don't tell Rahadin I told you. And don't take more than you can carry. It unbalances everything.
-> menu

~ guest
set court_ledger_read
Lief Lipsiege: The yellow room, third floor. The account is open: "G., sixteen, three gowns, all one size. Pending."
Lief Lipsiege [sad]: There was an "A." before her. Same size. That account is closed. I closed it myself. Neat hand. I always have a neat hand.
-> menu

~ rahadin
Lief Lipsiege [afraid]: He is very good at his work. I keep the count's accounts. He keeps the count's other accounts. His have more names in them.
-> menu

~ lief_inkwell_ok
set lief_inkwell_known
Narrator: Chained as he is, his fingers keep straying to the base of the heavy lead inkwell.
Lief Lipsiege [afraid]: Forty-one years at this desk, and once every winter I slipped a single copper piece underneath the lead.
Lief Lipsiege: There was a two-penny toll on the south road when I was a boy, and more for a cart.
Lief Lipsiege [weary]: It would be a poor thing to reach the gates at last and be turned back for want of the fare.
-> menu

~ lief_inkwell_miss
Narrator: He shifts the inkwell a single inch to the left and squares the stacked ledgers with both thumbs.
-> menu

~ free_offer
Lief Lipsiege [afraid]: Don't. Don't touch the chain. The rug. The count had it woven for this office. It does not like things to leave.
* We'll deal with the rug. Hold still. -> free_go
* Then we'll leave you to your sums. -> menu

~ free_go
Narrator: You set to the chain. Behind you, the rug ripples like a pond someone has dropped a stone in.
approve thistle +2 godrick_pendlebrook +1 wren_featherfoot +1: You took the chain off Strahd's accountant
combat court_accountant_rug

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- After the rug

~ after_rug
Narrator: The rug lies in rags. Lief stares at it, then at you, then at the shackle, which opens easily now that nothing is watching it.
Lief Lipsiege: Forty-one years, three months and a week. I shall have to close the column.
-> after_menu

~ after_menu
* Come with us as far as the gate. Vallaki could use a man who counts. -> go_vallaki
* [if not flag.court_treasury_known] Before you go: where's the count's money? -> vault_free
* [if flag.lief_inkwell_known and not flag.lief_toll_given] About those forty-one coins, for the gate. -> lief_toll
* You're free. Go wherever you like. -> go_free

~ lief_toll
set lief_toll_given
Lief Lipsiege: Take them, if you please, and give them to the man at the gate when you pass through.
Lief Lipsiege [weary]: My knees are not what they were, and I will be some time behind you on the road.
Lief Lipsiege [smile]: I shall keep the forty-first in my waistcoat, only so I do not walk out of this house a beggar.
Narrator: He counts forty dull coppers into your palm, each one worn smooth by decades of secret checking.
-> after_menu

~ vault_free
set court_treasury_known
Lief Lipsiege: Behind the lady in the long hall. Push the left side of the frame. There. Now I have stolen from him too. How very modern of me.
-> after_menu

~ go_vallaki
set lief_freed
attitude lief_lipsiege friendly
Lief Lipsiege: Vallaki. Their tithe books are a disgrace; I have seen them. Yes. Yes, I could do something with Vallaki.
Narrator: He tucks a ledger under each arm, thinks about it, and puts one back.
approve kip_smudgewick +1: You sent Lief Lipsiege to put Vallaki's books in order
-> END

~ go_free
set lief_freed
attitude lief_lipsiege friendly
Lief Lipsiege: Wherever I like. I shall have to make a list. I have never had to make that list.
Narrator: He goes out into the long hall, very carefully, as if the floor might send him a bill.
-> END
